      "target": "../sections/sec-000-introduction.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "As large language models (LLMs) continue to improve in agent-based scenarios, multi-agent systems\n(MAS) have become increasingly mature. Their core idea is to decompose complex problems into smaller\nsub-tasks, each of which is handled by a specialized expert agent [1]. Representative applications include\nVibe Coding [2] and Deep Research [3].Today, large-scale enterprise systems—such as those in finance,\nhealthcare, education, and cloud computing are also increasingly relying on multi-agent architectures\nto deliver accurate and eﬀicient problem solving capabilities. Despite their broad applica",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Introduction. Multi-agent systems split problems across specialised expert agents, and the router that picks the agent sets a floor on the whole system's performance. Routing is either performance-based (choosing model sizes to trade cost against accuracy, Figure 1a) or task-based (choosing domain experts, Figure 1b), and task-based routing, the enterprise backbone, suffers from agent conflicts: queries such as \"website latency\" in cloud computing may belong to several agents at once, yet single-label routers must pick one, and static routers cannot adapt as categories change. TCAR instead writes a reasoning chain and names every relevant agent, and a Refining Agent reconciles the selected agents' answers. The claimed contributions are a reasoning-centric router that outputs rationales and selects several agents (described as the first of its kind), the generate-then-refine pipeline for conflicts, and an open-source release; evaluation uses CLINC150, HWU64, MINDS14, SGD and a private cloud-computing dataset.",
