      "narrative": null,
      "content": "2\nTCAndon-Router\ndirections. Early approaches largely employed BERT-style encoders [20], formulating routing as a fixed\nsingle-label classification problem grounded in a predefined intent space and model structure. To support\nadding new agents without retraining, some studies shifted toward KNN-based methods, such as com-\nputing similarity between query and agent representations[21], or using query–agent dual-tower models\nor fusion-based cross-encoders [22] to dynamically compute relevance scores. [9]Train a binary classifier\nto determine whether the model performs excellently on a specific ta",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "The rest of Related Work, followed by the one-line opening of the Method section. Task routing moved from BERT-style single-label classifiers over a fixed intent space, to similarity and dual-tower or cross-encoder scoring that lets new agents be added without retraining, to LLM-based routers (ArchRouter, AgentRouter's knowledge-graph edge prediction, a confidence-driven router). The authors' criticism is that all still pick a single agent and give no structured explanation. Multi-agent frameworks such as AutoGen, CAMEL and CrewAI focus on how agents collaborate rather than which should take part, and pay little attention to merging several agents' outputs.",
