    {
      "id": "library/arxiv-2601.04544v1/blocks/prose-sec-004",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Dynamic Agent Addition",
      "pageStart": 3,
      "pageEnd": 3,
      "target": "../sections/sec-004-dynamic-agent-addition.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Traditional methods typically bind a router R to a fixed agent set A, when A changes R must be\nretrained. In TCAR, adding a new agent requires only appending anew to the existing set, yielding\nA′ = A ∪{anew} which produces a new description set D′ = D ∪{dnew}, The routing process then\nbecomes (Cq, Aq) = R(Prompt(q, D′)), Aq ⊆A, meaning that the router R itself does not need to be\nmodified or retrained to support newly added agents. This property greatly improves eﬀiciency and\nusability in real-world enterprise environments.\n3.3",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Dynamic agent addition. Because agents enter the router only through their descriptions, adding an agent means appending its description, D′ = D ∪ {dnew}; the router itself is neither modified nor retrained, which the authors present as the practical advantage over routers bound to a fixed agent set.",
