      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Multi-Agent Conflicts",
      "pageStart": 3,
      "pageEnd": 4,
      "target": "../sections/sec-005-multi-agent-conflicts.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Selecting Multiple Agents. Traditional task routing typically formulates routing as a single-label\nclassification problem:R(Prompt(q, A)) = ai ∈A meaning that each query can only be assigned to a sin-\ngle agent. This formulation implicitly assumes strict and mutually exclusive domain boundaries, as well\n3\nTCAndon-Router\nas a clearly defined intent space—assumptions that rarely hold in real enterprise environments. When\nmultiple agents are simultaneously suitable for handling a query due to overlapping domain responsi-\nbilities or cross-domain problem characteristics, an agent conflict occurs,",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "How TCAR handles agent conflicts, in three parts. First, routing is redefined from picking one agent to selecting a subset Aq ⊆ A with |Aq| ≥ 1, a conflict being the case |Aq| > 1. Second, the router reasons before it selects, analysing the query's likely causes, the technical stack and each agent's responsibilities; the stated benefits are more stable selection under overlap or ambiguity, and reasoning that engineers can inspect to fix routing errors or agent descriptions. Third, all selected agents answer in parallel and a Refining Agent filters and fuses their answers into one, yq = F({rij}), so that conflicts become multi-perspective evidence rather than a forced single choice.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
