      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Training TCAR",
      "pageStart": 4,
      "pageEnd": 6,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-training-tcar.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "To endow the router R with both multi-agent selection capabilities and interpretable reasoning, we adopt\na two-stage[28] training strategy. We first apply supervised fine-tuning (SFT)[29] to teach the model\nthe basic “reason–then–select” pattern. We then further enhance the quality of agent selection and the\nstability of reasoning through reward-based reinforcement learning using DAPO [30].\nSupervised Fine-Tuning. In the first stage, we train the model using labeled data with standard\ncausal language modeling. SFT equips the model with the following capabilities: (i) performing semantic\nalignm",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Training TCAR in two stages. Supervised fine-tuning with the standard autoregressive loss teaches the reason-then-select output, using a dedicated <reason> tag rather than <think>. Reinforcement learning with DAPO then improves selection. The reward applies to the agent set only and combines a precision-like term R1 = |Aq ∩ A∗| / max(1, |Aq|), a coverage term R2 = |Aq ∩ A∗| / max(1, |A∗|), and a length penalty β·max(|Aq| − |A∗|, 0) against listing too many agents, weighted by α and β. The prose describes R2 as all-or-nothing (paid only when Aq contains the whole gold set), but the formula given is the fractional recall. Figure 2 contrasts a solo agent with the Refining Agent pipeline.",
        "state": "draft",
